{"summary":"Verified an updated shortlist of three current EMBL group leaders, with separate evidence for research fit and PhD-recruitment status. Corrected the prior Bork entry: Peer Bork died on January 16, 2026 and must not be treated as a current group leader.","sections":[{"title":"Updated shortlist","body":"1. Nassos Typas — strongest host–pathogen and microbial-systems fit. The official Typas Group page describes bacterial cellular networks, pathogens, antibiotic resistance, microbiome interactions, and host–pathogen work including Salmonella infection. Typas is also listed on EMBL’s current Recruiting Group Leaders page, which identifies groups actively looking to hire PhD students. citeturn0search7turn0search6

2. Oliver Stegle — strongest single-cell/computational fit. The official Stegle page describes statistical and machine-learning methods for molecular variation, spatial and multi-omics data, and single-cell readouts. His Join Us page explicitly directs PhD applicants to the EMBL International PhD Programme and says the group is always looking for new talent. Stegle is listed among current recruiting group leaders. citeturn0search0turn0search4turn0search6

3. Jordi van Gestel — best additional microbial-interaction fit. His group combines quantitative single-cell microscopy, functional genomics, CRISPR libraries, experimental evolution, and modelling to study predator–prey microbial interactions, with explicit interest in the emergence of host–pathogen and host–symbiont interactions. His official Open Positions page welcomes PhD applicants, although the current EMBL central recruiting list does not list him; therefore, direct group-level openness is evidenced, but participation in the current central PhD recruitment round is not verified. citeturn1search1turn1search5turn0search6"},{"title":"Important correction","body":"The previous answer named Peer Bork as a current third option. That is no longer valid: EMBL announced that Peer Bork died on January 16, 2026. His historic research was relevant, but he should be removed from any current-faculty or current-student-recruitment shortlist. citeturn0search11"},{"title":"Recruitment interpretation","body":"Research fit and student acceptance are separate claims. For Typas and Stegle, there is current official evidence both of scientific fit and listing on EMBL’s recruiting-group page. For van Gestel, the group’s own Open Positions page welcomes PhD-level applicants, but the central EMBL recruiting list is preliminary and does not currently list him; this should be reported as group-level openness rather than confirmed participation in the current round. EMBL states that the recruiting-group list may change before interviews. citeturn0search6turn1search5"},{"title":"Programme timing","body":"As of August 8, 2026, EMBL states that 2026 PhD Summer Recruitment is closed and that the next application round opens in September 2026. citeturn0search10"}],"drafts":[],"follow_ups":["Prioritise Typas and Stegle for the next EMBL application round.","Treat van Gestel as a promising third contact but verify his participation in the September 2026 round before relying on 